#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# canonicalize_polygon

#% text
# `canonicalize_polygon(p)`: the polygon with its distinct corners sorted and the boundary as indices, anticlockwise

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
canonicalize_polygon(rectangle())
#% output
#= polygon([[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]], [1, 3, 4, 2])

#% text
# With `all` the parts of fewer than three corners are kept; with `full` their corners stay in the list but only the parts of three or more corners are indexed. A polygon in space keeps its corners in the order they first appear.

#% cell
canonicalize_polygon(polygon([[[1, 0], [0, 1], [-1, 0]], [[2, 2], [3, 3]]]), all)
#% output
#= polygon([[-1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [2, 2], [3, 3]], [[1, 3, 2], [4, 5]])

